When the layout of a Mach message isn’t one a transport builds, Ladybird
drops it and disconnects the peer. So does Blink: Its ChannelMac::
OnMachMessageReceived() reports kReceivedMalformedData to OnError() for
an unknown message ID, a descriptor of the wrong type, or a disposition
it doesn’t expect, and that closes the channel. Gecko’s ChannelMach::
ProcessIncomingMessage() returns false in the same cases, and the
channel then closes with OnChannelError(). And WebKit’s MachMessage
receive path returns ReceiveResult::InvalidMessage for them. Blink and
WebKit accept a null send right as an attachment, but Ladybird rejects
one, as its Rust transport already does, since Ladybird never sends one.
All of this comes from reading their sources, not from running them.
